{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6
    ,
      "why": "This is the family that owns the selection objective. It already produced the fleet's lowest feasible power_index (2.11) and 3 of the 18 merged-front points, so it is the only family that can push the min-power corner. Its risk is Fmax: with m>1 the combinational path grows and throughput_msps = Fmax x 1 must stay >= 50, which is why only 15/45 of its draws were feasible. I therefore keep m in 2..6 (small enough to plausibly hold Fmax >= 50, large enough to actually cut register count and power) and let the search find the m where Fmax crosses 50. data_width starts at 13 because the output LSB is 2^-(W-2) and max_abs_err <= 2^-12 is only reachable at W>=13 (W=13 is borderline, W=14+ gives rounding margin); n_iter 10..20 spans the convergence-limited low-power corner up to full-precision. angle_guard/frac_guard 0..3 and both rounding modes are cheap accuracy levers that let the search trade a few bits of datapath for fewer iterations."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4
    ,
      "why": "The fleet's evidence says pipelined is the workhorse of the merged front (15 of 18 points, 39/55 feasible, best power 4.21). It computes identical bits to pipelined_m for the same (W, N, guards, rounding), so it is the reliable way to populate the mid/high-accuracy part of the front where pipelined_m may be Fmax-infeasible. It is unlikely to win the min-power selection rule (its per-stage registers cost more than pipelined_m), but hypervolume rewards covering the whole curve, and its higher Fmax gives headroom at the accuracy end. Same accuracy ranges as pipelined_m so the two families are directly comparable point-for-point on the (power_index, accuracy_bits) plane."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Constraints are throughput_msps >= 50 and max_abs_err <= 2^-12; objectives are min power_index (selection rule) and max accuracy_bits (HV ref 12). Both my families are 1-result/cycle, so the throughput constraint reduces to Fmax >= 50 MHz, and accuracy is identical across families for the same datapath parameters. That makes this a pure register-count/power problem: pipelined_m with the largest m that still holds Fmax >= 50 should dominate pipelined, which is exactly what the fleet numbers show (best power 2.11 vs 4.21). So I weight pipelined_m 60% to attack the min-power corner the selection rule cares about, and pipelined 40% to keep the accuracy end of the front populated where pipelined_m's longer combinational path may fall below 50 MHz. Accuracy ranges are set from the LSB: output LSB = 2^-(W-2), so W>=13 is the hard floor for 2^-12 and W=14+ gives rounding margin; n_iter 10..20 covers both the convergence-limited low-power corner and full precision. Guards and rounding are included as cheap accuracy knobs. Round 1 spends the full 100-eval round on this split; later rounds should re-center m and W/N on the feasible boundary the first round reveals."
}
